# The Peios Kernel

The Peios kernel is the core of the Peios operating system. It builds on Linux's
hardware support and core services, with native access control, events,
configuration, layered storage and network policy integrated into the kernel.

The native subsystems are:

- **KACS:** tokens, security descriptors, access checks and process integrity.
- **KMES:** trusted event stamps, event emission and delivery rings.
- **LCS:** registry objects, layers, watches, transactions and source dispatch.
- **StrataFS:** a filesystem presenting an ordered stack of directories, with
  provider selection and copy-up governed by Peios authority.
- **NTFE:** packet policy evaluation, flow decisions and policy generations.

Start with the [architecture and reading guide](docs/kernel.product/100--introduction.topic/300--architecture-and-reading-guide.md)
for how these services fit together, and [Peios kernel behaviour](docs/kernel.product/100--introduction.topic/200--peios-behaviour.md)
for the changes to inherited Linux behaviour.

## Documentation

The maintained kernel documentation lives in [docs/](docs/), in Trail format.
The [documentation overview](docs/kernel.product/100--introduction.topic/100--overview.md)
provides routes into internals, development, administration, hardware and
userspace interfaces. Application and service developers can start with
[native kernel interfaces](docs/kernel.product/600--userspace.antho/100--native.antho/100--start-here.topic/100--overview.md).

`Documentation/` contains retained machine-consumed inputs and documentation
build integration. Edit explanatory articles in `docs/`; the
[documentation maintenance guide](peios/docs-migration/README.md) explains source
mapping, generated references and carrying upstream changes forward.

With Trail and the documentation plugin dependencies installed, build and check
the site from the repository root:

```sh
make htmldocs
make linkcheckdocs
```

Use `TRAIL=/path/to/trail` to select a binary. The default output directory is
`Documentation/output/`.

## Build and test

The repository contains the directly maintained kernel source. Bazel declares
build inputs and invokes Kbuild using a pinned container image. Read
[Building the Peios kernel](peios/README.md) for setup, outputs and qualification
details before running the commands below.

Kernel builds require an x86_64 Linux host, Python 3.11 or newer, Docker, and the
image selected in `peios/build-settings.json` already available in the local
Docker daemon. The Bazel launcher uses `curl` and `sha256sum` to download and
verify its pinned binary.

From the repository root:

```sh
./peios/bazel build //:kernel       # production configuration, development keys
./peios/bazel run //:kernel-dev     # incremental development build
./peios/bazel test //:kunit         # build and boot the KUnit configuration
```

Controlled kernel outputs appear under `bazel-bin/kernel/`. These commands
produce development artifacts: module archives are unsigned, and signing,
native integration qualification and release publication are separate stages.

## Working on the kernel

KACS, KMES and LCS live in `security/pkm/`; StrataFS in `fs/stratafs/`; NTFE's
networking integration in `net/ntfe/`; and native public interfaces in
`include/uapi/pkm/`. Peios build, test, packaging and maintenance tools live in
`peios/`. Changes to existing Linux subsystems are maintained alongside them.

Follow the [kernel coding style](peios/CODING_STYLE.md) and the workspace
`_styleguide` for shared engineering and Git conventions. The
[style command guide](peios/style/README.md) describes the required tooling.
Update the relevant articles in `docs/` when changing documented behaviour or
interfaces, and run the checks appropriate to the affected subsystem.

The `PEIOS KERNEL EXTENSIONS` entry in [MAINTAINERS](MAINTAINERS) identifies
the Peios maintainer and owned paths. Inherited Linux maintainer entries and
upstream contribution guides apply when working with those upstream projects.

